# Build context filter for `docker build -f apps/backend/Dockerfile … apps/backend`
# — the context is THIS directory (see the Dockerfile's header), so every
# pattern here is relative to `apps/backend/`, not to the repository root.
#
# This file replaces the repository-root `.dockerignore`, which was written for
# a root context that no longer exists. The entries that excluded sibling
# deployables (`apps/parent-app`, `apps/child-app`, `apps/admin-dashboard`) are
# gone: none of them is inside this context, so none of them can reach the
# image, and a pattern that cannot match is a claim nobody can check.
#
# Deliberately a DENY list, not an allow list: `**` + re-inclusions is clever,
# unreadable, and impossible to verify without a Docker daemon — which this
# repository's authoring environment does not have.

# Dependencies and build output — reinstalled/rebuilt inside the image. A host
# node_modules leaking into the context is how a sandbox-specific binary (or a
# broken Prisma shim) reaches a production image.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
tsconfig.build.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets and local state — never in a build context, never in a layer.
# `.env.example` is documentation; the image needs neither it nor a real .env.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/google-services.json

# Local runtime artefacts
**/dump.rdb
.evidence-store
**/coverage
**/.nyc_output
**/*.log

# Editors and OS droppings
.vscode
.idea
**/.DS_Store

# Test material: the production image runs no tests. (`tsconfig.build.json`
# already excludes `test` and `scripts` from the compile; this keeps them out
# of the uploaded context as well — `scripts/` holds the CI guards, which are
# ts-node executables that the application never imports.)
test
scripts
